{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-114339",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since we have an overall common factor of $2$, we start by dividing our equation through by $2.$ This gives \n\n$$\n4{u}^{3}-3{u}^{2}-36u+27=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{u}^{3}-3{u}^{2}-36u+27 & = \\\\ (4{u}^{3}-3{u}^{2})-(36u-27) & = \\\\ {u}^{2}(4u-3)-9(4u-3) & = \\\\ (4u-3)\\cdot {u}^{2}-(4u-3)\\cdot 9 & = \\\\ (4u-3)({u}^{2}-9) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve:\n\n- Setting the factor $(4u-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4u-3 & =0 \\\\ 4u & =3 \\\\ u & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({u}^{2}-9)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ {u}^{2} & =9 \\\\ u & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions to the given equation are $\\frac{3}{4}$ and $\\pm 3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
